  "dom_id": "step-q124625",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin\u2061\\theta .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ sin\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin\u2061\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$",
